parse_uint :: proc(text: string_view, base: int = 0) -> Result(uint, Parse_Error) ---;

/*
	Accepts `[+-]digits[.digits][(e|E)[+-]digits]`, where either digit run around
	the point may be empty but not both. Underscores may follow any digit, the
	rule `parse_u64` applies, in all three digit runs.

	The result is correctly rounded, and what `fmt` prints this parses to the
	same value. Parsing is in the "C" numeric locale, so a program that sets a
	decimal-comma locale for its own C code still parses `1.5` as one and a
	half. It allocates nothing, however long the text.

	Infinities and NaNs are not spellable; a caller that needs them tests for the
	words itself. A finite spelling past the largest `f64` is `Overflow`; one
	too small for the smallest subnormal rounds to a signed zero.

	Making that locale can fail, which panics here; `try_parse_f64` reports it.
*/
